this hanging mass is made entirely of  bees the swarm can hold itself together  even when shaken about by the wind now a  team of scientists have been finding out  how about once a year colonies of  honeybees leave their nests in swarms  searching for a new home until they find  a new site the bees rest in these  temporary clusters the clusters often  found in trees but this one is forming  in a lab at Harvard in Massachusetts  little do the bees know they're about to  go for a ride  the researchers built this shaking  apparatus to investigate how the bee  swarm reacts to movement when shaken  from side to side shearing forces act on  the colony with the bees at the top  taking the brunt of it as they try to  hold up everything beneath them the  forces threaten to destabilize the whole  colony so the bees do something clever  they spread out the flatter shape  changes the colonies center of mass and  so it wobbles less reducing the pressure  on the bees it makes sense imagine  you're standing up and you find the  ground shaking beneath you instinctively  you'd crouch down same principle anyway  what's particularly interesting here is  that the forces at play are acting on  the swarm as a whole but each bee can't  see the shape of the swarm so how does  it know where to go or what to do to  find out  researchers trapped the bees on the  outside of the swarm as the colony shook  they moved upwards to where the stress  is greatest  the researchers modeled this behavior  and found that if all the bees follow  the rule move towards the area of higher  stress the colony flattens out  increasing its stability and so  decreasing the average stress on each  bead great for the colony but individual  bees are actively putting themselves  under more stress to do it this kind of  altruistic behave  is not very common in nature except that  is in use social insects like bees and  ants which rely on altruism for survival  what's more even though each individual  bee doesn't necessarily know what's  going on by simply moving towards an  area of higher stress the colony as a  whole acts intelligently this is called  emergent intelligence and some  scientists think that it's through a  process like this that individual  neurons firing in our brain can  collectively come together to make well  us talk about a hive mind
 

